# Waveshare ESP32-P4-WIFI6-POE-ETH
#
# Deltas from the Waveshare ESP32-P4-ETH build (sdkconfig.defaults). Build it with:
#   idf.py -B build.poe -D SDKCONFIG=build.poe/sdkconfig \
#          -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;boards/poe_p4.defaults" build
#
# Why this board is worth a target: it is the only one so far that takes PoE, so
# a KVM in a rack needs one cable instead of two. Waveshare also link this
# project from the board's own documentation page, so people arrive with it in
# hand and expect it to work.
#
# Read off the published schematic (ESP32-P4-WIFI6-POE-ETH-Schematic.pdf), and
# the pleasant surprise is how little differs: Ethernet, microSD, the WiFi
# co-processor and the button all sit on the same GPIOs as the boards we already
# support, so almost everything here is inherited rather than declared.
#   Ethernet (IP101G): TX_EN 49, TXD0 34, TXD1 35, CRS_DV 28, RXD0 29, RXD1 30,
#                      MDC 31, MDIO 52, REF_CLK 50 - identical to the P4-ETH.
#   microSD: CLK 43, CMD 44, D0 39, D1 40, D2 41, D3 42 - identical to the
#            Function EV board, and the slot has a real power gate (below).
#   ESP32-C6 over SDIO: 14-19, with C6_CHIP_PU driven from GPIO 54 through a 0R.
#   BOOT button: GPIO 35, which is also Ethernet TXD1 - the same sharing that
#   makes the password reset read the pin once at start-up and hand it back.
#
# NOT YET VERIFIED ON HARDWARE. Configured from the schematic and published as a
# build so owners can try it, the same way the NANO and Guition targets started.

# --- flash and partitions ---------------------------------------------------
# 32 MB, like the P4-ETH, so the base defaults already fit. Nothing to say here.

# --- silicon revision -------------------------------------------------------
# Left at the base build's pre-3.0 setting, which is very likely also the truth
# here: every Waveshare P4 board we have seen carries rev 1.3 silicon, and both
# the NANO and the Guition turned out pre-3.0 when someone finally tested them -
# after we had guessed otherwise. The schematic does not name a revision.
#
# There is no forgiving choice here, which is worth knowing: the revisions are
# fenced off in BOTH directions. A pre-3.0 image declares max chip rev 1.99 and a
# rev-3 image declares min 3.0, so each refuses to start on the other's silicon.
# It is not "slower but working" - it is a board that sits in the bootloader.
#
# So a rev 3.x board needs the p4-poe-rev3 image, which CI publishes beside this
# one; there it also gets the faster capture path (UYVY straight into the
# encoder, ~22 fps at 1080p instead of ~15) and a writable microSD. These two
# lines are the whole difference, kept here for a local build:
# CONFIG_ESP32P4_SELECTS_REV_LESS_V3=n
# CONFIG_ESP32P4_REV_MIN_300=y

# --- updates ----------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_KVM_UPDATE_URL="https://espkvm.github.io/espkvm/firmware/p4-poe/manifest.json"

# --- WiFi co-processor ------------------------------------------------------
# An ESP32-C6-MINI-1 on the same SDIO pins the Function EV board uses, so the
# same esp-hosted configuration applies - including the guard against its eager
# constructor init, which otherwise races the microSD for the SD host.
CONFIG_KVM_WIFI=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_P4_DEV_BOARD_FUNC_BOARD=y
CONFIG_SLAVE_IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_CP_TARGET_ESP32C6=y
CONFIG_ESP_HOSTED_AUTO_CALL_INIT_BEFORE_APP_MAIN=n

# --- microSD ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Unlike the Function EV board, the card slot really is power-gated: GPIO 45
# drives an AO3401 P-channel MOSFET feeding SD1_VDD, so it is active LOW and the
# firmware can cycle a card that has wedged.
CONFIG_KVM_SD_PWR_GPIO=45

# --- console ----------------------------------------------------------------
# USB-C carries UART for flashing, and the serial-JTAG is the console.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y

# Which board this is: names it in the console and picks the expansion header
# the Pins tab draws.
CONFIG_KVM_BOARD_WAVESHARE_POE=y
